{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 5, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a major figure in the history of the Levant during the twelfth century. He served as the Count of Tripoli for many years and acted as a regent for the Kingdom of Jerusalem. His life was defined by complex political alliances and constant warfare with Muslim forces. Raymond was born into a powerful noble family that controlled significant territories along the Mediterranean coast. His early years were spent managing the affairs of his county while navigating the shifting powers of the region.\n\n*Early Life and Inheritance*\nRaymond inherited his title at a young age after the death of his father. This event placed a heavy burden on his shoulders because he had to defend the countship from external threats. He quickly proved his capability as a leader by securing his borders and maintaining internal stability. His education included training in military tactics and diplomatic negotiation which served him well in later years. The political landscape of the Crusader states required leaders who could balance local interests with the demands of European nobility. Raymond managed to build strong relationships with other Christian rulers while also engaging in truces with Muslim emirs when necessary.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nThe most significant period of his career occurred when he became regent for the young King Baldwin IV. This role placed him at the center of power within the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Raymond faced intense opposition from factions that wanted to exclude him from influence. He navigated these conflicts with caution and often found himself caught between rival groups. The leprosy of Baldwin IV complicated the succession issue and created a vacuum of power that Raymond tried to fill. His decisions during this time shaped the future of the kingdom and influenced the outcome of major battles. He supported various claimants to the throne and tried to maintain unity among the fractured nobility.\n\n*Later Years and Legacy*\nRaymond eventually retired from active politics and spent his final years in his own county. He died in 1187 leaving behind a legacy of diplomatic skill and political maneuvering. His death occurred just before the catastrophic Battle of Hattin which changed the course of the Crusades. The countship of Tripoli continued under his successors but the kingdom he helped protect faced imminent collapse shortly after his passing. Historians view him as a pragmatic leader who prioritized the survival of the Crusader states over ideological purity. His life story illustrates the difficulties faced by Christian rulers in the Holy Land during that tumultuous era. The region remained unstable even after his departure and the balance of power shifted dramatically in the following decades.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0022097188}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.7881409e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0047279727}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.090894595}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0074223876}, {"token": " major", "logprob": -3.837491}, {"token": " figure", "logprob": -0.16422111}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.040752355}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.0017202382}, {"token": " history", "logprob": -0.23506099}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -2.384186e-07}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -5.8175825e-05}, {"token": " Lev", "logprob": -3.2056243}, {"token": "ant", "logprob": -3.8028487e-05}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.00025520005}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -2.384186e-07}, {"token": " tw", "logprob": -0.00090866827}, {"token": "elfth", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.14155488}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.0025092824}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.23547517}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.002354232}, {"token": " Count",